mm: report the memory a guest may still plug

A guest given memory through virtio-mem as it needs it boots with little
and has the rest plugged on demand, up to what its host allows. sysinfo(2)
and /proc/meminfo report only what has been plugged so far, and programs
size themselves by that: Node.js's V8 sizes its heap by the smaller of the
memory it sees and its cgroup's limit, at 259 MiB in a guest booted with
256 MiB that may grow to 128 GiB, where a container on the host sizes it
at 4,144 MiB.

Add vm.pluggable_kbytes, which the guest's own agent sets to the memory the
host may plug from then on, and add what is still to be plugged to the
total, free and available memory that sysinfo(2) and /proc/meminfo report.
The kernel keeps the memory the guest has with all of it plugged, its
memory as the sysctl is written and the amount written, and each report
adds the difference from the memory that report read: memory plugged, or
unplugged, changes what it adds as it changes the memory it reads, so that
the total reported stays what the guest may reach, and reading the sysctl
gives what the reports add then. Nothing in the kernel's own accounting
counts it: si_meminfo() is as it was, and only the two reports add it.

